    "title": "Watervliet fire destroys two houses - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Kumi Tucker ,\n10 hours ago\nWatervliet, N.Y. (WNYT) – Firefighters say the fire on 7th Avenue between 19th and 23rd Streets had a great head start on them.\n“What I heard was like a snap like a crackle and that’s when I really noticed the smoke,” said neighbor Sharon Vandenburgh.\nThe smoke could be seen from far away.\n“The wind hurt us,” said Watervliet Fire Chief Tom Garrett. “The wind spread that fire fast on us. Even now you feel the breeze. The wind hurt us.”\nThe fire started in one house and spread to another. The chief says there were two apartments in each.\nFirefighters from Watervliet, Watervliet Arsenal, Green Island, Troy, and Cohoes responded.\nCrews faced challenges.\n“Just the volume of fire,” said Chief Garrett. “It was all in the back. We had to get to the back of the building. Gangways, you had fire blowing out the windows, so we ended up bringing an engine company down in the alley so we could get hose lines on that fire.”\nThis is a lot of activity on what is usually a quiet street.\n“I saw from my living room window,” said neighbor Gabrielle Kennedy. “I heard sirens and I saw one of the cops drive down the opposite way of the one way and when I looked out, there was just a bunch of smoke and then once the fire trucks came they were putting water on it, the flames got bigger.”\nPeople say this is a place where neighbors look out for neighbors.\nCrews worked to keep the flames from spreading to the Troy Bowling Association building next door and to another house on the other side.\n“I ran out,” said Vandenburgh. “I noticed that there was major water. My concern was the families– that was my concern.”\nAuthorities say at least eight adults and one child are displaced.\nOne resident has a newborn. She said her baby is six weeks old.\nThere is a GoFundMe for two women who lived in one house. http://Fundraiser by Autumn Dennis : Help Dee Olivia & Mari Rebuild After Total Loss Fire\nOne of them was home and, with a cousin, grabbed what they said mattered most: their four dogs. They got out safely, but now face a tough road ahead.\n2",
